academy-id "q-66005" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+10=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-6$" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{3}$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=10$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=6$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation I has no solutions." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-66005/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80449399-3ae4-593f-8c3b-00bcdcc99727"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-66005"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120608/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "77c3f371-635a-5627-8c62-7908b1827c03" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120608/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "58523245-3c13-52aa-acdd-341e48494df3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120608/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "a6a9f750-348c-526e-89f4-a6c9536475c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120608/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "38d0a89c-b099-5c34-932b-0c07d7988348" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120608/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "1ac1f845-b6a4-5a6e-b1a0-9daf8ace3a94" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120608/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "055dd466-cbe5-56b2-82e2-75d11ba56017" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120608/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-120608/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-120608/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-120608/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-120608/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-120608/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120608" :question/id #uuid "793f1730-c52c-50dc-ae88-11311bf4fb2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-120608" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-2$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{4}$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{5}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equations II and III have no solutions." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-120608/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80449399-3ae4-593f-8c3b-00bcdcc99727"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120608"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3533"] :question/problem "Solve the equation $2\\csc x+4=0$ for $0 < x <   2\\pi.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{(-2)} = -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain $0 < x <   2\\pi.$ To get a value inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\nx_1 = x + 2\\pi = -\\dfrac \\pi 6 + 2\\pi = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the  fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Since $\\sin{x}<    0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/85b7b73fa793153cfedb2ffdb6825edd0845f93dbb472404862a78476a56fc35.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4262"] :question/problem "Find, in radians to $1$ decimal place, all of the solutions to the equation $\\dfrac{\\csc x}{5} -1 = 0$ for $0 \\leq x <              2\\pi.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{5}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})=0.201\\,357…≈0.201\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.201$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 0.201.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 0.201.$ Since $\\sin{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 0.201.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/8bcd1ea412f9235a330f37ee9059a023d0ce9e03e2c38388875dc0c12afebc1e.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -0.201 \\\\  & ≈2.941\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to one decimal place) are $x = 0.2, 2.9.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3535"] :question/problem "Solve the equation $2\\csc x + 4 = 0$ for $0 < x \\leq   \\dfrac{7\\pi}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{(-2)} = -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi.$\n\n$$\nx_1 = x+2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + 2\\pi = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6.\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the  fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Since $\\sin{x}<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R =\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/85b7b73fa793153cfedb2ffdb6825edd0845f93dbb472404862a78476a56fc35.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the remaining solutions in the given interval, we need to find angles that are coterminal with $x_1$ or $x_2.$ To do this, we add integer multiples of $2\\pi$ until all solutions are found:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & ={x}_{1}+2\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2\\pi =\\frac{23\\pi }{6} \\\\ {x}_{4} & ={x}_{2}+2\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2\\pi =\\frac{19\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{23\\pi}{6}$ is outside the required domain, so we ignore it.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6},\\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{19\\pi}{6}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4263"] :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{2}{5}$ for $-2\\pi \\leq x <           2\\pi.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} =\\dfrac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe equation above has no solution because the sine function always satisfies $-1 \\leq \\sin{x} \\leq 1,$ and the value $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ is outside of this range.\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{2}{5}$ has no solution." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
